  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 238 AM EDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\n===== Sunshine Returns For Most Today =====\n\nThe upper trough axis is swinging east of the Ohio Valley this \nmorning, and will eventually result in clearing skycover for most of \nour area. Clouds are expected to linger through most of the day for \nthe I-75 corridor counties, but dry air filtering in will leave us \ndry today. Temps will be cool given the strong CAA regime we are, \nwith highs today only in the 50s. \n\n===== Frost and Freeze Headlines for Sunday AM =====\n\nBy tonight, upper ridging will build east across the central US, and \nsfc high pressure will move directly over our area. This will \nsupport strong radiational cooling after an already cool day, with \ntemps Saturday night and into Sunday morning dropping down into the \n30s. Majority of the forecast area is under a Frost Advisory for \nSunday morning, but a few counties in the Bluegrass, including \nFayette/Lexington, are in a Freeze Warning. That area has the \nhighest chance for sub-freezing temps. \n\nAfter the chilly start, Sunday will feature plenty of sunshine and \ntemps climbing into the 60s by the afternoon hours. \n\n===== Dry Weather Expected All of Next Week =====\n\nThe upper trough currently passing over our area today will \neventually slide east off the mid-Atlantic coast by Sunday and \ndeepen into a closed low just off the coast of Virgina/North \nCarolina. This feature will become stalled and meander over that \narea for a several days, possibly until the end of next week, \nresulting in the upstream pattern to amplify the upper ridging into \na large dome of high pressure over the central US. This will cause \nour weather to be dry over the next week, with temps gradually \nwarming back up to normal in the 70s, though possibly slightly above \nnormal by the end of the week as we creep back into the upper 70s.",
